{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_lnx7dnmemrvxalezcq7xax5ulrivvqng6vtsphv3sl4oemuvjyaq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All four phases done, with one real bug found and fixed.\n\n## The bug\n\n`fallcalc`'s allocation-failure branch was **unreachable in the Rust release build**:\n\n```\n[E9] DIVERGENCE with fallcalc(0,0,0,0) and malloc(20) forced to fail:\n     C = -1,  Rust = 368\n```\n\nAt `-O2`+, LLVM recognized `fallcalc`'s *constant-size* `malloc(5 * sizeof(int))` whose pointer never escapes and applied **heap-to-stack promotion**: the allocation became an `alloca`, which cannot fail, so `if (data_array == NULL) return -1;` was folded away as dead code. Disassembly confirmed only 1 of the 2 `malloc` call sites survived. Fixed by routing both allocations through a private `c_malloc` helper that hides the size and result behind `core::hint::black_box`, preserving the real libc call and the NULL check at every optimization level.\n\nThe dev profile alone reported everything green — only the profile sweep exposed this.\n\n## Phases\n\n**A** — `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md` (15 rows), `CONFIGS.md` (35 rows), derived mechanically. Notably, `lib.h` declares only `fallcalc`, but `lib.c` defines **six** non-`static` functions that all land in `.dynsym`; all six are tested directly, not just the wrapper.\n\n**B** — All 35 configuration rows pass with fixed-seed randomized inputs, including a composition cross-check that rebuilds `fallcalc` from *one* library's low-level exports and compares against the *other* library's whole.\n\n**C** — All 15 error rows pass with exact sentinel assertions. E7/E9 (`malloc == NULL`) were made reachable by interposing `malloc` from the test executable — fair because the Rust `.so` deliberately imports libc `malloc` rather than using Rust's allocator, so one interposer hits both.\n\n**D** — Symbol diff empty (exactly 6 in each, verified after clean rebuild). 33 configurations × 55 tests, 0 failures.\n\nTwo corrections were to my own tests, not the translation: `param4 % 10 == -1` yields size `0` (a `malloc(0)` success, not a failure), and the C source uses **CRLF**, so `$`-anchored `sed` patterns silently never matched.\n\n## Confidence checks\n\nBecause passing tests prove nothing if the suite cannot fail, `mutation_check.sh` compiles 25 deliberately-broken copies of the C (in `$TMPDIR` — `c_src/` untouched, hashes verified) and requires rejection of each: **25 caught, 0 missed** under both dev and release.\n\nTwo mutants initially appeared \"missed\": relaxing `d >= (double)INT_MAX` to `>`. Rather than paper over them, I brute-forced 5,173,210 doubles with exhaustive one-ULP sweeps at both thresholds — **0 differences**. They are genuinely *equivalent* mutants (at exactly `(double)INT_MAX`, the fallthrough `(int)d` already yields `INT_MAX`), not blind spots, and are now labelled as such so the score stays honest.\n\n`tests/soak.rs` adds 13.75M randomized cases on the rounding-sensitive float paths, ruling out FMA contraction and reassociation differences.\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: the `black_box` fix relies on an optimization *hint*, which is the standard tool but not a hard language guarantee. The `errors_malloc_failure` test is what pins the behavior, and it runs across all six optimized configurations — so a future toolchain regression would be caught rather than silently reintroduced."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1226,"cache_read_input_tokens":159320,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1226,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9949b9a0-2bb6-4848-9988-6d43859b4a13","uuid":"bd262ab2-94f6-40c8-a801-36cb89b4807e","timestamp":"2026-08-28T01:13:08.275Z"}
